---
import DocsLayout from '../../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeExample from '../../../../components/CodeExample.astro';

const title = 'Accessibility and CSS';
const description = 'How accessibility, CSS architecture, focus states, reduced motion, logical properties, and progressive enhancement work together.';

const reducedMotionExample = `/* Import this partial into layer(components).
 * State changes work immediately unless motion is opted into. */
.status-message {
    background-color: var(--c-surface);
}

.status-message.is_loading {
    background-color: var(--c-surface-alt);
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .status-message {
        transition: background-color var(--transition-s);
    }
}`;
---

<DocsLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    schemaType="TechArticle"
>
    <article>
        <p class="eyebrow">Guide</p>

        <h1>
            Accessibility and CSS
        </h1>

        <p>
            Accessibility is not a QA checkbox added at the end of delivery.
            It starts in the CSS architecture itself.
        </p>

        <p>
            Focus visibility, reduced motion, readable spacing, logical
            properties, colour contrast, and keyboard-safe interaction patterns
            all depend on how styles are organised.
        </p>

        <h2>
            Where CSS causes accessibility problems
        </h2>

        <ul>
            <li>focus styles removed with no replacement</li>
            <li>hover-only interactions</li>
            <li>animation that ignores reduced motion preferences</li>
            <li>fixed dimensions that break zoom and text resize</li>
            <li>visual order that fights source order</li>
            <li>theme overrides that damage colour contrast</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>
            How LSCSS helps
        </h2>

        <ul>
            <li>state classes keep interaction states explicit</li>
            <li>theme layers isolate visual overrides safely</li>
            <li>shallow selectors reduce override chaos</li>
            <li>logical properties improve international support</li>
            <li>rare utilities (e.g. screen-reader helpers) stay separate from layout styling</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>
            Respect reduced motion
        </h2>

        <p>
            Start with complete, immediate state changes. Add transitions and
            animations inside <code>prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference</code>{' '}
            in the owning component layer. This keeps the same content and controls
            available when motion is reduced or the media query is unsupported.
        </p>

        <CodeExample code={reducedMotionExample} language="css" />

        <p>
            A global <code>animation-duration: 0s</code> rule does not disable
            transitions, smooth scrolling, or JavaScript-driven motion. Review
            those separately, include animated pseudo-elements, and never make
            completing an animation a condition for opening a control or updating
            its accessible state. Loading status still needs visible text; colour
            alone does not communicate it.
        </p>

        <p>
            See the <a href="/apply/browser-support/#ordered-css">popover and disclosure examples</a>{' '}
            for native controls with capability checks and optional motion.
            In projects with custom media, define a named preference query in
            settings and expand it through the same build as other media queries.
        </p>

        <h2>
            Start here
        </h2>

        <p>
            Align <a href="/apply/base-styles/">base styles</a> and <a href="/apply/modifiers-and-state/">modifiers and state</a>{' '}
            with your product’s focus and keyboard model, then audit contrast and motion
            on real pages—not only in isolation demos.
        </p>

        <h2>
            Recommended reading
        </h2>

        <ul>
            <li><a href="/apply/methodology/">Core methodology overview</a></li>
            <li><a href="/apply/base-styles/">Base styles and global defaults</a></li>
            <li><a href="/apply/theme-layer/">Theme layer</a></li>
            <li><a href="/apply/modifiers-and-state/">State classes</a></li>
        </ul>

        <p>
            Good accessibility usually looks boring. That is often a sign it is
            working properly.
        </p>
    </article>
</DocsLayout>
